00:16:02,850 --> 00:16:07,049
cern, potentially centralizing high risk
research or setting global

230
00:16:07,129 --> 00:16:11,929
standards, and strengthening whistleblower
protections through international
agreements to

231
00:16:11,929 --> 00:16:15,049
encourage reporting of safety concerns
within the AI industry.

232
00:16:15,529 --> 00:16:18,470
For more information on these topics,
please read the

233
00:16:18,470 --> 00:16:21,750
next chapter on AI governance. The
textbook has a

234
00:16:21,750 --> 00:16:26,110
supplementary note titled Is AI Governance
Useful, Desirable, and

235
00:16:26,110 --> 00:16:31,350
possible? Which we'll skip over here. Risk
Management Risk

236
00:16:31,350 --> 00:16:35,870
management ensures that risks minus
mitigations remain below tolerance

237
00:16:35,870 --> 00:16:42,350
levels. The core equation is risks,
mitigations, tolerance. This

238
00:16:42,350 --> 00:16:48,019
operational process connects everything
else. Governance sets rules, evaluations

239
00:16:48,019 --> 00:16:52,740
measure capabilities, safety culture
establishes norms, but risk management

240
00:16:53,059 --> 00:16:55,700
makes the daily decisions about whether to
pause training,

241
00:16:56,019 --> 00:16:59,539
which mitigations to implement, and how to
maintain acceptable

242
00:16:59,539 --> 00:17:04,019
risk levels throughout development. The
framework has four interconnected

243
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:10,099
identification, analysis, treatment and
governance. Each addresses a distinct

244
00:17:10,099 --> 00:17:14,299
question about managing AI risks
systematically. There is figure

245
00:17:14,299 --> 00:17:18,619
3.25 in the textbook. Here it depicts
overview of

246
00:17:18,619 --> 00:17:22,819
the risk management framework. Risk
identification determines what could

247
00:17:22,819 --> 00:17:27,619
go wrong. First, classify known risks from
literature covering

248
00:17:27,619 --> 00:17:33,099
domains like cybersecurity, CBRN
manipulation, autonomous replication and
loss

249
00:17:33,099 --> 00:17:38,900
of control, with any exclusions clearly
justified. Second, identify

250
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:42,420
unknown risks through both internal and
external red teaming

251
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:47,200
that explores beyond predefined
categories. Third, create risk models

252
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:50,359
that map step by step pathways from AI
capabilities

253
00:17:50,519 --> 00:17:55,079
to real world harms validated by
independent experts. The

254
00:17:55,079 --> 00:17:57,680
goal is to understand, not just what bad
things

255
00:17:57,680 --> 00:18:01,480
could happen, but specifically how they
could materialize. Risk

256
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:06,559
analysis translates abstract concerns into
measurable thresholds. Start by

257
00:18:06,559 --> 00:18:09,619
setting a risk tolerance, the maximum harm
level you'll

258
00:18:09,619 --> 00:18:14,619
accept, preferably expressed
quantitatively as probability times
severity per

259
00:18:14,619 --> 00:18:18,779
time period. Then operationalize this into
key risk indicators

260
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:25,019
capability thresholds like scores 60% on
cyber offense benchmark

261
00:18:25,339 --> 00:18:31,059
paired with key control indicators.
Mitigation targets like maintain

262
00:18:31,059 --> 00:18:36,759
security level 3 and achieve 99.9%
jailbreak resistance. The

263
00:18:36,759 --> 00:18:40,599
pairing creates if then statements. If
this KRI threshold

264
00:18:40,599 --> 00:18:43,759
is crossed, then these KCI targets must be
met.

265
00:18:44,319 --> 00:18:48,319
Organizations must commit to pausing
development if required. KCIs

266
00:18:48,319 --> 00:18:52,559
cannot be achieved. Risk Treatment
implements and monitors mitigation

267
00:18:52,559 --> 00:18:57,359
measures. This has two phases. First,
implement mitigations across

268
00:18:57,359 --> 00:19:04,349
three containment measures controlling
access through information, security
deployment

269
00:19:04,349 --> 00:19:09,430
measures, preventing misuse through
safeguards and assurance processes
providing

270
00:19:09,430 --> 00:19:14,230
evidence of safety for advanced systems.
Second, continuously monitor

271
00:19:14,230 --> 00:19:18,670
against predetermined thresholds, tracking
both KRIs to detect when

272
00:19:18,670 --> 00:19:23,549
dangerous capabilities emerge and KCIs to
verify mitigations remain

273
00:19:23,549 --> 00:19:28,029
effective. Results should be transparently
shared with stakeholders and

274
00:19:28,029 --> 00:19:31,109
systems should monitor for novel risks
that weren't identified

275
00:19:31,109 --> 00:19:36,109
initially. The textbook includes figure
3.26. Here it depicts

276
00:19:36,109 --> 00:19:39,430
the three lines of defense model 3 LOD as

277
00:19:39,430 --> 00:19:44,309
described by the IIA Institute of Internal
Auditors. Risk

278
00:19:44,309 --> 00:19:47,549
Governance establishes who does what and
who verifies how

279
00:19:47,549 --> 00:19:50,670
it's done. Most industries use a three
lines of

280
00:19:50,670 --> 00:19:54,470
defense model. The first line consists of
operational managers

281
00:19:54,470 --> 00:19:58,359
who build systems and own daily risk
decisions. The

282
00:19:58,359 --> 00:20:01,799
second line includes specialized risk
teams that advise and

283
00:20:01,799 --> 00:20:05,920
challenge business decisions. The third
line is internal audit,

284
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:09,160
independent from peer pressure reporting
to the board to

285
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:13,479
verify the system works. Beyond this
structure, Governance includes

286
00:20:13,479 --> 00:20:17,559
board level oversight, risk culture
throughout the organization and

287
00:20:17,559 --> 00:20:21,720
external transparency about risks and
decision making processes. An

288
00:20:21,720 --> 00:20:25,240
extremely detailed analysis of current
risk management and safety

289
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:29,660
practices is conducted by Safer AI
available here. Every

290
00:20:29,660 --> 00:20:34,740
category identification, analysis,
treatment and governance is further
decomposed

291
00:20:34,740 --> 00:20:38,220
into extremely granular metrics that are
tracked and analyzed

292
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:41,539
to give risk ratings to all frontier AI
organizations.

293
00:20:41,939 --> 00:20:45,500
At this point, the textbook shows Figure
3.27. It

294
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:48,819
depicts risk management ratings of the
frontier AI safety

295
00:20:48,819 --> 00:20:54,599
organizations as of October 2025. Section
Safety culture Safety

296
00:20:54,599 --> 00:20:59,240
culture means building organizations where
people consistently prioritize safety

297
00:20:59,240 --> 00:21:02,720
over speed and where safety concerns can
actually change

298
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:06,880
decisions. This is a strategy for
preventing AI accidents

299
00:21:07,119 --> 00:21:11,799
through organizational design rather than
just technical fixes. Risk

300
00:21:11,799 --> 00:21:15,720
management is seen in many fields
including aerospace, nuclear

301
00:21:15,720 --> 00:21:19,859
power and financial services. Each of
these domains has

302
00:21:19,859 --> 00:21:25,019
developed sophisticated approaches to
identifying, analyzing and mitigating
potential

303
00:21:25,019 --> 00:21:28,859
harms. We want to mitigate AI safety
failures that

304
00:21:28,859 --> 00:21:33,140
stem from human and organizational factors
rushing to deploy

305
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:37,700
under tested systems, ignoring warning
signs, or creating incentives

306
00:21:37,700 --> 00:21:42,180
that reward moving fast over being
careful. Integrating Safety

307
00:21:42,180 --> 00:21:46,099
culture addresses these root causes by
changing how organizations

308
00:21:46,099 --> 00:21:49,720
operate. There is figure 3.28 in the
textbook. Here

309
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:53,119
it depicts the AI Safety Index report for
summer

310
00:21:53,119 --> 00:21:57,519
2025. The scores show whether each
company's governance structure

311
00:21:57,519 --> 00:22:01,480
and day to day operations prioritize
meaningful accountability for

312
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:04,839
the real world impacts of its AI systems.
This

313
00:22:04,839 --> 00:22:09,160
includes things like whistleblowing
systems, legal structures, and advocacy

314
00:22:09,160 --> 00:22:13,240
efforts related to AI regulations. The AI
industry lacks

315
00:22:13,240 --> 00:22:17,710
the professional safety culture found in
traditional engineering. Fields

316
00:22:17,710 --> 00:22:21,430
like civil and mechanical engineering have
professional ethics codes,

317
00:22:21,509 --> 00:22:26,230
safety margins, and reliability
engineering as standard practice. AI

318
00:22:26,230 --> 00:22:30,029
development emerged from mathematics and
computer science, which, outside

319
00:22:30,029 --> 00:22:33,910
of safety critical software systems have
weaker safety traditions.

320
00:22:34,630 --> 00:22:38,710
Unlike other industries where workers
directly experience safety risks,

321
00:22:38,869 --> 00:22:42,349
AI developers rarely face the consequences
of their systems

322
00:22:42,349 --> 00:22:45,740
failures. This distance makes it harder to
build the

323
00:22:45,740 --> 00:22:49,180
shared understanding of risk that
motivates safety culture. In

324
00:22:49,180 --> 00:22:52,660
other fields, AI safety culture must be
forward looking

325
00:22:52,740 --> 00:22:57,220
rather than reactive. Most industries
developed safety cultures after

326
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:02,059
major disasters, nuclear power after Three
Mile island, healthcare

327
00:23:02,059 --> 00:23:06,299
after decades of preventable deaths.
Waiting for AI disasters

328
00:23:06,299 --> 00:23:09,019
would be irresponsible since some failures
might not be

329
00:23:09,019 --> 00:23:13,460
survivable. The aerospace industry
demonstrates how safety culture can

330
00:23:13,460 --> 00:23:18,700
transform entire fields through systematic
practices. Aviation moved from

331
00:23:18,700 --> 00:23:22,500
frequent crashes to extraordinary safety
records not just through

332
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:26,779
better technology, but through cultural
changes like mandatory incident

333
00:23:26,779 --> 00:23:31,900
reporting, blame, free safety
investigations, and standardized
procedures that

334
00:23:31,900 --> 00:23:36,339
prioritize safety over schedule pressure.
AI companies can adopt

335
00:23:36,339 --> 00:23:41,569
similar practices, systematic incident
reporting, regular safety training, and

336
00:23:41,569 --> 00:23:46,009
organizational structures that ensure
safety concerns reach decision makers

337
00:23:46,009 --> 00:23:49,769
with authority to act. Strong safety
culture has three

338
00:23:49,769 --> 00:23:56,569
observable leadership, accountability,
systematic processes, and psychological
safety for

339
00:23:56,569 --> 00:24:02,210
raising concerns. NIST's AI risk
management framework identifies executive

340
00:24:02,369 --> 00:24:07,119
leadership taking personal responsibility
for AI risk decisions, diverse

341
00:24:07,119 --> 00:24:11,400
teams informing risk management throughout
development, and systematic processes

342
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:15,000
for incident reporting and information
sharing across the organization.

343
00:24:15,480 --> 00:24:20,119
Organizations with safety culture
implement systematic processes where
safety

344
00:24:20,119 --> 00:24:23,680
considerations are built into standard
workflows rather than being

345
00:24:23,680 --> 00:24:27,559
optional add ons. They create environments
where employees can

346
00:24:27,559 --> 00:24:31,319
raise safety concerns without career
penalties and where such

347
00:24:31,319 --> 00:24:35,890
concerns visibly influence decisions. As
one example, NASA learned

348
00:24:35,890 --> 00:24:40,210
that technical excellence isn't enough.
The Challenger disaster occurred

349
00:24:40,289 --> 00:24:44,410
partly because engineers safety concerns
didn't reach decision makers

350
00:24:44,410 --> 00:24:49,089
due to organizational dynamics that
discouraged dissent. Safety culture

351
00:24:49,089 --> 00:24:53,490
extends beyond individual projects to
encompass hiring, performance evaluation,

352
00:24:53,569 --> 00:24:58,849
and organizational incentives. Companies
serious about safety culture evaluate

353
00:24:58,849 --> 00:25:03,009
candidates for safety mindset during
hiring, include safety metrics

354
00:25:03,009 --> 00:25:06,130
in performance reviews, and ensure that
safety work is

355
00:25:06,130 --> 00:25:09,450
recognized and rewarded rather than seen
as slowing down

356
00:25:09,609 --> 00:25:14,009
real progress. This includes providing
dedicated time and resources

357
00:25:14,009 --> 00:25:16,930
for safety work, not treating it as
something teams

358
00:25:16,930 --> 00:25:21,009
should squeeze in around other priorities.
Organizations with a

359
00:25:21,009 --> 00:25:25,930
strong safety culture maintain detailed
incident reporting systems, conduct

360
00:25:25,930 --> 00:25:30,009
regular safety assessments and demonstrate
continuous improvement in their

361
00:25:30,009 --> 00:25:33,569
safety practices. They invest in training
programs that go

362
00:25:33,569 --> 00:25:37,849
beyond compliance checklists to develop
genuine safety expertise across

363
00:25:37,849 --> 00:25:42,450
teams. Most importantly, they create
feedback loops where safety

364
00:25:42,450 --> 00:25:46,089
information flows both up and down the
organization, enabling

365
00:25:46,089 --> 00:25:50,170
rapid learning and adaptation when new
risks emerge. Weak

366
00:25:50,170 --> 00:25:54,019
safety culture means we see safety
WASHINGTON the appearance

367
00:25:54,019 --> 00:25:58,900
of caring about safety without substance
Organizations with weak

368
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:02,380
safety cultures often have safety policies
on paper but

369
00:26:02,380 --> 00:26:05,140
don't follow them. When under pressure,
they may blame

370
00:26:05,140 --> 00:26:09,619
individuals for accidents rather than
examining systemic causes. They

371
00:26:09,619 --> 00:26:12,980
typically treat safety work as overhead
that slows down

372
00:26:12,980 --> 00:26:16,859
real progress, leading to under resourcing
and marginalization of

373
00:26:16,859 --> 00:26:21,380
safety teams. Safety concerns in these
organizations rarely change

374
00:26:21,380 --> 00:26:25,549
actual deployment decisions. We talk more
about safety washing

375
00:26:25,549 --> 00:26:26,990
in the Challenges section.
